y delivery-v1 contracts classify repository, external or mixed delivery during clarification and plan review, separately from execution authorization. External ticket content/relationship work uses configured Linear/Taskbot integrations, persisted before-state/intents, safe retry reconciliation, independent complete-state verification, snapshot-bound human acceptance and a final state check. External work bypasses publication, code-diff fanout, CI and merge; repository safeguards remain. Mixed work requires external proof and every confirmed repository merge. Lifecycle status/comments and PR links remain runtime-owned and excluded from external fingerprints. Done synchronization rechecks external evidence; tracking-only retries do not replay task work. Saved definitions remain frozen; authenticated reviewed recovery of an idle failed draft-pr run verifies already-applied external work before a new human gate and preserves original history/checkpoint/conversations. Every workflow accepts the same retained repository scope. Repositories with identical nonempty routing-label sets appear as one project in the manual composer; shared labels route issues to all matching repositories. Manual launches may pass repositoryIds alongside the primary repositoryId. A grouped workspace contains one worktree per repo. Factory and Takeover publish and review one PR/MR per changed repository, preserve per-repo delivery receipts through retries, bind human approval to every exact URL/head, and complete only after all delivered revisions are confirmed merged. Unchanged repos remain context. Custom scripts/exec run once in the shared workspace with FACTORY_REPOSITORIES and tool input repositories exposing the scope. Factory publication, takeover, CI readiness, handoff and SHA-guarded merge use the repository Git provider. GitHub and GitLab (including explicit self-managed hosts) are built in; other hosts use repositories[].gitProvider custom executable adapters. Public hosts and existing githubUrl/gitlabUrl fields auto-detect. Unknown hosts require explicit provider selection. Runs retain accepted provider coordinates/adapter on retry and recovery. GitHub delivery uses the built-in REST/GraphQL client; the GitHub CLI is optional. Local first launch opens guided project/installed-agent selection without enrollment or login. Direct HTTP loopback dashboard and API access requires an actual loopback socket peer, exact loopback authority and same-origin browser context; forwarding headers never grant access. Remote HTTPS origins retain separate passkey sessions. Connect GitHub in protected Project setup to save a private token for API delivery and managed Git authentication; explicit execution profiles retain their accepted repository-scoped credential bindings. For token-free API requests from agent commands, save a JSON request containing method, path and optional body, then run bobs-factory github-api --repo <selected GitHub repository URL> --request <request file>. Native Git/SSH/forge credential stores remain host-owned. Human approval and provider-confirmed merge still gate completion. Handoff routes unresolved guide requirements and delivery gaps through the configured fixer, rebuilds the guide and rechecks readiness. Repeated unresolved gaps at an unchanged revision wait for specific assistance; an answer does not waive scope or approve delivery. Runtime coordinates finalization of overlapping repository/base scopes after publication while implementation remains parallel; assistance and human waits release admission. Runtime supervises queued CI and bounded retries of concretely identified infrastructure failures without holding an agent; unknown failures still require diagnosis. Review results explicitly declare completed, blocked or failed: an empty findings list alone cannot approve. Scoped context tools expose applied limits and pagination; large role outputs can submit validated revision-bound artifacts. Run provenance identifies actual runtime/workflow/contract/instruction receipts and preserves unknown historical identity. Newly authored technical/mixed review guides require whole-PR scope classification, 3–6 system-map lanes and chapter part links; only evidenced purely visual scope is exempt. Saved older guides remain readable. New stock Factory/Takeover runs support video-v1: select up to three authentic headless demonstrations when useful, validate media and revision provenance including linked QA story definitions, inspect playback, then embed accepted clips lazily with transcripts in guides and inspector. Optional recording failures remain limitations; explicit required recordings stay blocked. Legacy/frozen runs and custom recipes opt in only explicitly. Videos supplement screenshots and fresh QA; human approval remains required. Media is loopback-only and bounded (120 seconds/50 MiB per clip; 512 MiB per run), with startup cleanup of current and historical capture assets for terminal runs after 30 days and abandoned temporary files after 24 hours. The review reader keeps item commenting throughout and collects feedback, additional feedback and page-level PR/diff actions on Decide, reached through the sticky feedback count. The Factory dashboard requires passkey sessions on every address, including localhost. An empty authentication store shows operator-authorized first-passkey setup; provider webhooks and OAuth use their separate listener. New stock Factory and Takeover runs extract a versioned requirement inventory before six independently configurable specialist reviewers run through fanout. Coverage-required fanout needs exactly one coverage-v1 supplier; replace it explicitly before removing it. Reviewers share a runtime-frozen clean revision and context baseline; incomplete or stale results cannot approve. Called workflows retain their review association in parent guides, QA, subsequent fanout branches and approval steps across restart; QA uses configured aggregate provenance even when gates are renamed. Settled findings require fresh evidence to reopen across intervening omissions. Recipes edits affect new launches; accepted runs and customized legacy reviewers keep their frozen contracts. Executable QA, screenshots and explicit revision-bound human approval remain required. Refinement questions can include generated questionRecommendations with zero-based questionIndex, answer and evidence-based reason. The dashboard selects suggestions by default and provides a separate blank Custom answer field. Recommendations require explicit Send answers submission or a ticket reply; they never automatically authorize work. Legacy questions without metadata use blank text fields. Changed questions or recommendations notify the originating ticket once per batch; unchanged restored waits retain notification deduplication. Factory manual launches accept an originating Linear or Taskbot ticket URL as the prompt or explicit ticket source. The runtime fetches complete ticket context, retains verified tracker identity through roles and follow-ups, and owns publication and PR links. Native Linear operational progress, questions and blockers use its readable agent transcript; durable developer documentation and confirmed delivery summaries remain issue comments. Taskbot retains milestone comments. Missing manual Linear transcript configuration leaves delivery visibly pending. Coding tickets stay nonterminal during work, In Review awaiting human/provider action, and Done only after provider-confirmed merge. Ticket synchronization failures remain visible in run activity and ticketSync receipts; retry only tracking through POST /api/runs/:id/ticket-sync after restoring access or reassessing conflicts. Simple retains its native tracking lifecycle. Factory supports opt-in Web Push through Notifications for new questions, review revisions, human-only blockers, failures needing help and successful completions. Enable and test each browser device explicitly; remote disable 